1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,971
This is ECE 358, video 5 a. The topic is the application layer and HTTP connections. It covers chapter 2, slides 1 to 23.

2
00:00:13,421 --> 00:00:20,631
After this video, you can explain the two application architectures, processes, sockets and port numbers.

3
00:00:21,081 --> 00:00:24,153
You can name the transport needs of an application.

4
00:00:24,603 --> 00:00:30,065
You can draw non-persistent and persistent HTTP on a time diagram.

5
00:00:30,515 --> 00:00:36,467
And you can explain why one object takes 2 RTT plus the file transmission time.

6
00:00:36,917 --> 00:00:42,677
You can also answer question bank questions 1 to 20 of the application layer.

7
00:00:43,127 --> 00:00:51,553
A network application is a set of programs. The programs run on different end systems, and they communicate over the network.

8
00:00:52,003 --> 00:00:56,249
For example, web browser software talks to web server software.

9
00:00:56,699 --> 00:01:01,499
The routers in the network core do not run normal user applications.

10
00:01:01,949 --> 00:01:06,023
A router uses only the network, link and physical layers.

11
00:01:06,473 --> 00:01:15,988
So an app developer writes software only for the application layer of the end systems. This allows fast development and spread of new apps.

12
00:01:16,438 --> 00:01:22,027
The bits go down the stack of the sender, through the router, and up the stack of the receiver.

13
00:01:22,477 --> 00:01:28,109
But at the application layer, the message goes from one end system to the other end system.

14
00:01:28,559 --> 00:01:33,317
Some apps face the user, such as the Web, e-mail and video streaming.

15
00:01:33,767 --> 00:01:38,887
Other apps do not face the user, such as DNS and DHCP.

16
00:01:39,337 --> 00:01:46,931
BGP and RIP also run as applications, on routers. They create and update the routing tables.

17
00:01:47,381 --> 00:01:58,581
There are two architectures for an application. The first is client-server. In it, the server is an always-on host. It is ready to accept service requests.

18
00:01:59,031 --> 00:02:01,506
The server has a permanent IP address.

19
00:02:01,956 --> 00:02:05,263
Servers are often in data centers, for scaling.

20
00:02:05,713 --> 00:02:09,126
Clients contact the server and communicate with it.

21
00:02:09,576 --> 00:02:11,603
A blue arrow is a request.

22
00:02:12,053 --> 00:02:14,314
A green arrow is the response.

23
00:02:14,764 --> 00:02:20,396
Clients can be intermittently connected, and they can have dynamic IP addresses.

24
00:02:20,846 --> 00:02:26,841
Clients do not communicate directly with each other. All communication goes through the server.

25
00:02:27,291 --> 00:02:35,909
Examples of client-server protocols are HTTP, IMAP for mail access, and FTP for file transfer.

26
00:02:36,359 --> 00:02:42,439
The second architecture is peer-to-peer, or P2P. There is no always-on server.

27
00:02:42,889 --> 00:02:51,188
Arbitrary end systems communicate directly. Peers request service from other peers, and provide service in return.

28
00:02:51,638 --> 00:03:01,771
Slide 7 gives an example. N peers want to download a file F from a source. The slide writes the size of F as F between two bars.

29
00:03:02,221 --> 00:03:08,408
We use our own numbers: N is 4 peers, and the file size is 100 megabytes.

30
00:03:08,858 --> 00:03:15,706
Each peer downloads only a fraction, f N, from the source. f N is the file size over N.

31
00:03:16,156 --> 00:03:21,681
That is 100 megabytes over 4, which is 25 megabytes for each peer.

32
00:03:22,131 --> 00:03:25,630
Then the peers exchange their own fractions with each other.

33
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:33,589
Each peer gets the other 3 parts from the other peers: 3 times 25, which is 75 megabytes.

34
00:03:34,039 --> 00:03:36,621
Now every peer has a full copy of F.

35
00:03:37,071 --> 00:03:43,983
This is self scalability. Each new peer brings new service demands, but also new service capacity.

36
00:03:44,433 --> 00:03:51,899
The cost: peers are intermittently connected and can change IP addresses. So management is complex.

37
00:03:52,349 --> 00:03:57,448
Examples are BitTorrent for file sharing, and the RIP protocol on routers.

38
00:03:57,898 --> 00:04:08,373
Hosts do not communicate. Processes communicate. A process is a program that runs in a host. It is one execution instance of a program.

39
00:04:08,823 --> 00:04:17,164
Two processes in the same host use inter-process communication, or IPC. The operating system defines it.

40
00:04:17,614 --> 00:04:23,907
Examples of IPC are files, pipes, message queues, shared memory and semaphores.

41
00:04:24,357 --> 00:04:29,776
Processes in different hosts communicate by exchanging messages over the network.

42
00:04:30,226 --> 00:04:34,749
The process that initiates the communication is the client process.

43
00:04:35,199 --> 00:04:39,359
The process that waits to be contacted is the server process.

44
00:04:39,809 --> 00:04:47,915
P2P apps also have client processes and server processes. Some P2P processes act as both.

45
00:04:48,365 --> 00:04:55,533
A process sends and receives messages through its socket. The figure shows two hosts with their layer stacks.

46
00:04:55,983 --> 00:04:59,802
A process runs at the application layer of each host.

47
00:05:00,252 --> 00:05:05,329
The socket is the door between the application process and the transport layer.

48
00:05:05,779 --> 00:05:09,171
The app developer controls everything above the socket.

49
00:05:09,621 --> 00:05:13,632
The operating system controls everything below the socket.

50
00:05:14,082 --> 00:05:24,685
The sending process writes its message out of the door. It relies on the transport infrastructure to deliver the message to the socket of the receiving process.

51
00:05:25,135 --> 00:05:28,569
So two sockets are involved, one on each side.

52
00:05:29,019 --> 00:05:36,742
Slide 9 also lists the socket system calls. Socket creates a socket and returns a socket descriptor.

53
00:05:37,192 --> 00:05:42,909
The server calls bind to assign an address to its socket: an I P address and a port.

54
00:05:43,359 --> 00:05:47,626
Listen makes the socket a passive socket. It waits for connections.

55
00:05:48,076 --> 00:05:54,263
The client calls connect. The server calls accept, and accepts the connection from the client.

56
00:05:54,713 --> 00:05:57,145
Then send and receive move the data.

57
00:05:57,595 --> 00:05:59,323
Close closes the socket.

58
00:05:59,773 --> 00:06:08,114
To receive messages, a process must have a global identifier. A host has a unique 32-bit IP address.

59
00:06:08,564 --> 00:06:14,345
For example, 129 dot 97 dot 56 dot 100.

60
00:06:14,795 --> 00:06:24,673
Each of the 4 numbers is one group of 8 bits. For example, 129 is 1 0 0 0 0 0 0 1.

61
00:06:25,123 --> 00:06:28,045
4 times 8 bits is 32 bits.

62
00:06:28,495 --> 00:06:36,346
The IP address of the host is not enough to identify the process. Many processes can run on the same host.

63
00:06:36,796 --> 00:06:43,025
So each process also has a port number. The port number is an operating system concept.

64
00:06:43,475 --> 00:06:49,150
Well-known port numbers are reserved. An HTTP server uses port 80.

65
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:52,395
A mail server uses port 25.

66
00:06:52,845 --> 00:06:58,775
The tuple, IP address and port number, uniquely identifies a process on a host.

67
00:06:59,225 --> 00:07:15,695
So an HTTP message to the web server of w w w dot e c e dot u waterloo dot c a goes to IP address 129 dot 97 dot 56 dot 100, and port 80.

68
00:07:16,145 --> 00:07:24,785
An application-layer protocol defines four things. First, the types of messages, for example request and response.

69
00:07:25,235 --> 00:07:34,877
Second, the message syntax: the fields in the message, and their positions. The figure shows the first line of an HTTP request.

70
00:07:35,327 --> 00:07:38,143
A space, s p, separates the fields.

71
00:07:38,593 --> 00:07:43,607
Third, the message semantics: the meaning of the information in each field.

72
00:07:44,057 --> 00:07:45,998
The first field is the method.

73
00:07:46,448 --> 00:07:48,517
The second field is the object.

74
00:07:48,967 --> 00:07:51,037
The third field is the version.

75
00:07:51,487 --> 00:07:57,780
Fourth, the rules for when and how processes send messages and respond to messages.

76
00:07:58,230 --> 00:08:10,710
Open protocols are defined in R F Cs. Everyone has access to them, so different products can interoperate. Examples are HTTP and SMTP.

77
00:08:11,160 --> 00:08:15,448
Proprietary protocols, such as Skype and Zoom, are not open.

78
00:08:15,898 --> 00:08:22,469
An application chooses a transport service by its needs. The first need is data integrity.

79
00:08:22,919 --> 00:08:26,545
Bits can arrive with an error, or a packet can be lost.

80
00:08:26,995 --> 00:08:30,857
Packets can also arrive out of order, or arrive twice.

81
00:08:31,307 --> 00:08:37,067
File transfer and web transactions need 100 percent reliable transfer.

82
00:08:37,517 --> 00:08:39,693
Audio can tolerate some loss.

83
00:08:40,143 --> 00:08:46,287
The second need is throughput. Multimedia apps need at least a minimum throughput to be effective.

84
00:08:46,737 --> 00:08:49,809
Elastic apps use whatever throughput they get.

85
00:08:50,259 --> 00:08:57,192
The third need is timing. Internet telephony and interactive games need a low delay to be effective.

86
00:08:57,642 --> 00:09:02,634
The fourth need is security, for example encryption and data integrity.

87
00:09:03,084 --> 00:09:12,151
Question bank question 14 asks for three needs without security. So the answer is data integrity, throughput and timing.

88
00:09:12,601 --> 00:09:24,633
Now the question bank, application layer, questions 1 to 7. The question bank has no published answers, so these answers come from the slides. Pause the video and try them.

89
00:09:25,083 --> 00:09:32,592
Question 1 asks for five standard protocols and their functions. HTTP moves web objects.

90
00:09:33,042 --> 00:09:34,983
SMTP moves e-mail.

91
00:09:35,433 --> 00:09:39,209
DNS translates a host name to an IP address.

92
00:09:39,659 --> 00:09:43,201
DHCP gives a host its IP address.

93
00:09:43,651 --> 00:09:45,741
FTP transfers files.

94
00:09:46,191 --> 00:09:52,421
Other correct answers are IMAP for mail access, and BGP and RIP for routing tables.

95
00:09:52,871 --> 00:10:06,929
Question 2 asks for seven apps that users use directly. Slide 4 lists the Web, e-mail, text messaging, social networking, streaming video, multi-user games and video conferencing.

96
00:10:07,379 --> 00:10:12,115
Question 3. The two architectures are client-server and peer-to-peer.

97
00:10:12,565 --> 00:10:23,040
Question 4. A server process waits to be contacted. Its host is always on, has a permanent IP address, and is often in a data center.

98
00:10:23,490 --> 00:10:36,610
Question 5. A client process initiates the contact. Its host can be intermittently connected and can have a dynamic IP address. Clients do not talk directly to each other.

99
00:10:37,060 --> 00:10:48,729
Question 6. P2P has no always-on server. Peers talk directly, request and provide service, and give self scalability. Management is complex.

100
00:10:49,179 --> 00:10:54,683
Question 7. Examples are BitTorrent for file sharing, and RIP on routers.

101
00:10:55,133 --> 00:10:58,952
Questions 8 to 14. Pause the video and try them.

102
00:10:59,402 --> 00:11:08,042
Question 8. In the same host, processes use IPC: files, pipes, message queues, shared memory and semaphores.

103
00:11:08,492 --> 00:11:14,081
Question 9. In different hosts, the basic mechanism is to exchange messages.

104
00:11:14,531 --> 00:11:27,801
Question 10. A socket is the door between the process and the transport layer. The sender writes its message into its socket. Transport delivers the message to the socket of the receiving process.

105
00:11:28,251 --> 00:11:34,523
Question 11. A process is identified by the pair: IP address and port number.

106
00:11:34,973 --> 00:11:40,967
Question 12. A port number is an operating system number for a process on the host.

107
00:11:41,417 --> 00:11:45,855
Question 13. An HTTP server uses port 80.

108
00:11:46,305 --> 00:11:49,099
A mail server uses port 25.

109
00:11:49,549 --> 00:11:54,840
Question 14. The three needs are data integrity, throughput and timing.

110
00:11:55,290 --> 00:12:01,839
Now the Web. A web page consists of objects. The page has a base HTML file.

111
00:12:02,289 --> 00:12:05,724
The base file includes references to other objects.

112
00:12:06,174 --> 00:12:14,622
An object can be an HTML file, a JPEG or GIF image, a Java applet, an audio file, and so on.

113
00:12:15,072 --> 00:12:21,621
Each object can be on a different web server. For example, an ad can come from another server.

114
00:12:22,071 --> 00:12:26,317
Each object has a URL, a uniform resource locator.

115
00:12:26,767 --> 00:12:30,991
The URL has two parts. The first part is the host name.

116
00:12:31,441 --> 00:12:35,174
The second part is the path name of the object on that host.

117
00:12:35,624 --> 00:12:45,331
HTTP, the hypertext transfer protocol, is the application-layer protocol of the Web. It uses the client-server model.

118
00:12:45,781 --> 00:12:53,205
The client is a browser, such as Firefox or Safari. It requests, receives, and displays web objects.

119
00:12:53,655 --> 00:12:59,649
The server is a web server, such as Apache. It sends objects in response to requests.

120
00:13:00,099 --> 00:13:12,281
HTTP uses TCP. First, the client creates a socket and opens a TCP connection to the server, port 80. The server accepts the connection.

121
00:13:12,731 --> 00:13:17,488
Second, the browser and the server exchange HTTP messages.

122
00:13:17,938 --> 00:13:20,711
Third, the TCP connection is closed.

123
00:13:21,161 --> 00:13:27,753
HTTP is stateless. The server keeps no information about past client requests.

124
00:13:28,203 --> 00:13:34,027
The reason: protocols that keep state are complex. They must keep the past history.

125
00:13:34,477 --> 00:13:42,541
And if the server or the client crashes, their views of the state can be different. Then the two views must be reconciled.

126
00:13:42,991 --> 00:13:52,634
There are two types of HTTP connections. Non-persistent HTTP sends at most one object over a TCP connection.

127
00:13:53,084 --> 00:14:00,935
Persistent HTTP sends many objects over a single TCP connection between the client and that server.

128
00:14:01,385 --> 00:14:07,145
Slide 18 has an example. The client needs 4 objects from the same server.

129
00:14:07,595 --> 00:14:15,680
With non-persistent HTTP, the client first opens a TCP connection. That takes 3 messages.

130
00:14:16,130 --> 00:14:18,413
Then it downloads the first object.

131
00:14:18,863 --> 00:14:22,447
Then it closes the connection. That takes 2 messages.

132
00:14:22,897 --> 00:14:31,110
The client must do this again for the second, the third and the fourth object. So multiple objects need multiple connections.

133
00:14:31,560 --> 00:14:39,987
With persistent HTTP, the client opens one connection, downloads all four objects, and then closes it.

134
00:14:40,437 --> 00:14:49,397
Count the control messages. Non-persistent: 4 connections, each with 3 plus 2 messages. 4 times 5 is 20.

135
00:14:49,847 --> 00:14:55,052
Persistent: one connection with 3 plus 2 messages, which is only 5.

136
00:14:55,502 --> 00:15:08,067
To open a TCP connection, TCP in the client and TCP in the server exchange control information by message passing. The open takes 3 messages.

137
00:15:08,517 --> 00:15:12,976
After this exchange, they are ready for reliable data transfer.

138
00:15:13,426 --> 00:15:15,837
Then data flows in both directions.

139
00:15:16,287 --> 00:15:20,639
At the end, the connection is closed. The close takes 2 messages.

140
00:15:21,089 --> 00:15:29,835
Chapter 3 gives the details of TCP. For HTTP, you need only the time cost, which the next slides show.

141
00:15:30,285 --> 00:15:45,923
Slides 20 and 21 give an example of non-persistent HTTP. The user enters the URL w w w dot some school dot e d u slash some department slash home dot index.

142
00:15:46,373 --> 00:15:51,194
The base file contains text and references to 10 JPEG images.

143
00:15:51,644 --> 00:15:57,703
In step 1 a, the client initiates a TCP connection to the server at port 80.

144
00:15:58,153 --> 00:16:05,001
In step 1 b, the server waits at port 80. It accepts the connection and notifies the client.

145
00:16:05,451 --> 00:16:17,547
In step 2, the client sends an HTTP request message into its socket. The message says that the client wants the object some department slash home dot index.

146
00:16:17,997 --> 00:16:27,746
In step 3, the server receives the request. It forms a response message that contains the object, and sends the message into its socket.

147
00:16:28,196 --> 00:16:32,015
In step 4, the server closes the TCP connection.

148
00:16:32,465 --> 00:16:38,673
In step 5, the client receives the response with the HTML file and displays it.

149
00:16:39,123 --> 00:16:44,755
The client parses the HTML file and finds 10 referenced JPEG objects.

150
00:16:45,205 --> 00:16:50,431
In step 6, steps 1 to 5 repeat for each of the 10 JPEG objects.

151
00:16:50,881 --> 00:16:55,916
So the page needs 1 plus 10, which is 11 TCP connections.

152
00:16:56,366 --> 00:17:05,881
First, a definition. The RTT, the round trip time, is the time for a small packet to travel from the client to the server and back.

153
00:17:06,331 --> 00:17:12,539
The client initiates a TCP connection. The first RTT opens the connection.

154
00:17:12,989 --> 00:17:17,661
The second RTT starts with the HTTP request for the file.

155
00:17:18,111 --> 00:17:24,852
The second RTT ends when the first bits of the HTTP response return to the client.

156
00:17:25,302 --> 00:17:27,990
Then the server must transmit the whole file.

157
00:17:28,440 --> 00:17:35,096
The file transmission time, F T T, is the file size divided by the transmission rate.

158
00:17:35,546 --> 00:17:39,258
The last bit arrives one F T T after the first bit.

159
00:17:39,708 --> 00:17:47,153
The response time runs from the start of the TCP set up until the client has completely received the file.

160
00:17:47,603 --> 00:17:59,017
Add the parts: RTT, plus RTT, plus F T T. So the non-persistent response time per object is 2 RTT plus F T T.

161
00:17:59,467 --> 00:18:09,045
An example with our own numbers: the RTT is 40 milliseconds. The file is 1 megabit, which is 10 to the power 6 bits.

162
00:18:09,495 --> 00:18:15,575
The rate is 100 megabits per second, which is 10 to the power 8 bits per second.

163
00:18:16,025 --> 00:18:25,967
So the F T T is 10 to the power 6 over 10 to the power 8 seconds. That is 0.01 seconds, or 10 milliseconds.

164
00:18:26,417 --> 00:18:31,857
The response time is 2 times 40, plus 10, which is 90 milliseconds.

165
00:18:32,307 --> 00:18:38,344
Non-persistent HTTP has issues. It needs 2 RTTs per object.

166
00:18:38,794 --> 00:18:42,357
And the OS has overhead for each TCP connection.

167
00:18:42,807 --> 00:18:46,903
To save time, browsers often open parallel connections.

168
00:18:47,353 --> 00:18:58,275
The first bar shows a base file B and 3 referenced objects. Each object needs an open RTT, a request RTT, and its file time.

169
00:18:58,725 --> 00:19:06,469
So the total is 4 times 2 RTT plus F T T. That is 8 RTT plus 4 F T T.

170
00:19:06,919 --> 00:19:16,690
With persistent HTTP, the server leaves the connection open after it sends a response. Later messages use the open connection.

171
00:19:17,140 --> 00:19:25,289
If the client sends one request at a time, each referenced object needs only one RTT, for its request.

172
00:19:25,739 --> 00:19:36,278
The total is 2 RTT plus F T T, plus 3 times RTT plus F T T. That is 5 RTT plus 4 F T T.

173
00:19:36,728 --> 00:19:45,773
Better, the client sends a request as soon as it finds a referenced object. Then one RTT is enough for all the referenced objects.

174
00:19:46,223 --> 00:19:56,591
The total is 2 RTT plus F T T, plus RTT, plus 3 F T T. That is 3 RTT plus 4 F T T.

175
00:19:57,041 --> 00:20:08,327
In this drawing, F T T is 0.5 RTT. Non-persistent takes 8 plus 4 times 0.5, which is 10 RTT.

176
00:20:08,777 --> 00:20:19,486
Persistent with all requests at once takes 3 plus 4 times 0.5, which is 5 RTT. So the response time is cut in half.

177
00:20:19,936 --> 00:20:24,736
Question bank questions 15 to 20. Pause the video and try them.

178
00:20:25,186 --> 00:20:39,202
Question 15. The elements of a web page are objects. There is a base HTML file, and referenced objects, such as images, applets and audio files. Each object has a URL.

179
00:20:39,652 --> 00:20:45,241
Question 16. A web client requests, receives and displays web objects.

180
00:20:45,691 --> 00:20:53,371
Question 17. A web server accepts TCP connections, and sends objects in response to requests.

181
00:20:53,821 --> 00:20:57,128
Question 18. Both use HTTP.

182
00:20:57,578 --> 00:21:12,319
Question 19. The client opens a TCP connection to port 80 of the server. The server accepts it. Then HTTP messages flow over the connection, and at the end the connection is closed.

183
00:21:12,769 --> 00:21:25,036
Question 20. Non-persistent HTTP sends at most one object per connection. It costs 2 RTTs per object and OS overhead for each connection.

184
00:21:25,486 --> 00:21:34,937
Persistent HTTP keeps the connection open for many objects. It needs as little as one RTT for all referenced objects.

185
00:21:35,387 --> 00:21:44,475
Now the recap. One. There are two architectures: client-server, with an always-on server, and P2P, with self scalability.

186
00:21:44,925 --> 00:21:53,181
Two. A process is identified by its IP address and port number. It sends and receives messages through its socket.

187
00:21:53,631 --> 00:21:58,921
Three. Applications need data integrity, throughput, timing, and security.

188
00:21:59,371 --> 00:22:11,809
Four. HTTP is a client-server protocol over TCP, port 80. It is stateless. Non-persistent HTTP sends at most one object per connection.

189
00:22:12,259 --> 00:22:33,955
Five. One object takes 2 RTT plus the file transmission time. Persistent HTTP needs as little as one RTT for all referenced objects. Video 5 b solves the response time questions and reads the HTTP messages. That is the end of video 5 a.
